WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.064
How can we tell whether two networks were drawn
from the same underlying distribution?

00:00:06.144 --> 00:00:10.462
This paper compares two statistical tests based on
subgraph counts.

00:00:10.542 --> 00:00:14.399
The first uses the raw subgraph densities, or graph
moments;

00:00:14.479 --> 00:00:19.650
the second converts them into graph cumulants, a
combinatorial analogue of mean,

00:00:19.730 --> 00:00:21.750
variance, and skew for networks.

00:00:21.830 --> 00:00:26.082
Through theory, simulation, and real
genetic-interaction networks,

00:00:26.162 --> 00:00:31.332
the authors show that graph cumulants deliver
markedly higher statistical power,

00:00:31.412 --> 00:00:33.367
at no extra computational cost,

00:00:33.447 --> 00:00:37.436
and even work when only a single graph is observed
per sample.

00:00:37.516 --> 00:00:43.671
Their recommendation is simple: whenever you
analyze networks with subgraph or motif densities,

00:00:43.751 --> 00:00:46.690
use the corresponding graph cumulants instead.

00:00:47.070 --> 00:00:50.149
Suppose you are handed two collections of networks,

00:00:50.229 --> 00:00:52.874
each sampled from some unknown distribution,

00:00:52.954 --> 00:00:59.192
and asked a deceptively simple question: were they
generated by the same process or by different ones?

00:00:59.272 --> 00:01:03.527
The nodes carry no shared labels, so you cannot
match them one to one;

00:01:03.607 --> 00:01:06.624
all you can use are the statistics of their edges.

00:01:06.704 --> 00:01:09.845
This is the two-sample testing problem for networks,

00:01:09.925 --> 00:01:16.410
and it underlies tasks from comparing brain
connectomes to distinguishing biological interaction networks.

00:01:16.790 --> 00:01:20.264
Analysts commonly summarize a network by counting
small substructures,

00:01:20.344 --> 00:01:23.818
called subgraphs or motifs.

00:01:23.898 --> 00:01:27.372
But the raw densities of these motifs are heavily
entangled: the density of an edge is

00:01:27.452 --> 00:01:30.926
strongly correlated with the density of a wedge,

00:01:31.006 --> 00:01:34.480
and so on.

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:38.034
This redundancy weakens any test built directly on
the densities,

00:01:38.114 --> 00:01:41.588
and many existing methods must resort to costly
resampling to judge whether an observed difference is significant.

00:01:41.668 --> 00:01:45.142
The authors ask whether a better set of coordinates,

00:01:45.222 --> 00:01:48.696
one that removes lower-order redundancy,

00:01:48.776 --> 00:01:52.250
can make these tests both stronger and cheaper.

00:01:52.630 --> 00:01:55.405
The paper's central contribution is a simple,

00:01:55.485 --> 00:02:00.608
drop-in two-sample test for networks that swaps
graph moments for graph cumulants.

00:02:00.688 --> 00:02:04.796
Using theory, controlled simulation, and real
biological networks,

00:02:04.876 --> 00:02:11.649
the authors show this swap consistently increases
statistical power without changing the computational cost.

00:02:11.729 --> 00:02:18.438
They further show that the cumulant test statistic
tracks a known chi-squared distribution remarkably well,

00:02:18.518 --> 00:02:20.659
even with only a handful of graphs,

00:02:20.739 --> 00:02:24.148
so false-positive rates can be controlled analytically.

00:02:24.228 --> 00:02:29.478
Strikingly, the cumulant test even works when just
one graph is observed per sample,

00:02:29.558 --> 00:02:32.270
a regime where the moment test is undefined.

00:02:32.650 --> 00:02:37.471
Both tests follow the same three-step recipe:
choose a maximum subgraph order,

00:02:37.551 --> 00:02:41.555
estimate each sample's distribution from its
subgraph statistics,

00:02:41.635 --> 00:02:44.760
and measure the distance between the two estimates.

00:02:44.840 --> 00:02:47.901
That distance is the squared Mahalanobis distance,

00:02:47.981 --> 00:02:52.425
which weights differences by an analytically
computed covariance matrix.

00:02:52.505 --> 00:02:57.201
The only difference between the tests is the
coordinates: raw graph moments,

00:02:57.281 --> 00:03:01.976
which are injective-homomorphism subgraph
densities, versus graph cumulants.

00:03:02.056 --> 00:03:08.511
Cumulants are obtained from moments by a
combinatorial Möbius transform over all connectivity-respecting

00:03:08.591 --> 00:03:10.584
partitions of a subgraph's edges,

00:03:10.664 --> 00:03:16.490
capturing the excess propensity of a structure
beyond what its smaller pieces already predict.

00:03:16.570 --> 00:03:22.145
Because computing the covariances only needs
subgraph counts up to twice the chosen order,

00:03:22.225 --> 00:03:25.350
both tests share the same computational complexity.

00:03:25.730 --> 00:03:27.412
To compare the tests fairly,

00:03:27.492 --> 00:03:31.502
the authors first run a controlled competition on
synthetic data.

00:03:31.582 --> 00:03:34.522
They pit a heterogeneous stochastic block model,

00:03:34.602 --> 00:03:38.234
which has an uneven degree distribution but no
communities,

00:03:38.314 --> 00:03:42.450
against an assortative one, which has communities
but even degrees,

00:03:42.530 --> 00:03:46.918
holding edge density fixed so the two are genuinely
hard to tell apart.

00:03:46.998 --> 00:03:54.342
They vary graph size from one hundred twenty-eight
to two hundred fifty-six nodes and the number of graphs per sample.

00:03:54.422 --> 00:03:59.376
They then move to real data: genetic-interaction
networks of Arabidopsis, mouse,

00:03:59.456 --> 00:04:02.082
human, and rat from the FunCoup repository,

00:04:02.162 --> 00:04:07.430
again matched in edge density so that separating
them demands higher-order structure.

00:04:07.810 --> 00:04:09.334
The results are decisive.

00:04:09.414 --> 00:04:11.002
On synthetic block models,

00:04:11.082 --> 00:04:17.995
the test using cumulants achieves a consistently
higher area under the ROC curve than the test using moments,

00:04:18.075 --> 00:04:22.358
and the advantage widens as the number of graphs
per sample shrinks.

00:04:22.438 --> 00:04:26.593
The moment test breaks down entirely below four
graphs per sample,

00:04:26.673 --> 00:04:29.608
where its covariance estimate becomes singular,

00:04:29.688 --> 00:04:34.292
while the cumulant test keeps working all the way
down to a single graph.

00:04:34.372 --> 00:04:39.488
The same pattern holds on real biological networks:
with edge density held equal,

00:04:39.568 --> 00:04:43.595
adding more subgraph orders steadily sharpens the
cumulant test,

00:04:43.675 --> 00:04:47.380
whereas it makes the moment test overfit and
perform worse.

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:52.094
A clean ablation over subgraph order tells the
story on real data.

00:04:52.174 --> 00:04:54.636
Using only single edges, at order one,

00:04:54.716 --> 00:04:59.719
neither test can separate the density-matched
networks, exactly as expected.

00:04:59.799 --> 00:05:04.602
Adding two-edge subgraphs already tips the balance
in favor of cumulants.

00:05:04.682 --> 00:05:09.083
Adding three-edge subgraphs pushes the cumulant
test further ahead,

00:05:09.163 --> 00:05:12.962
while the moment test starts to overfit and slip backward.

00:05:13.042 --> 00:05:18.714
Sweeping the block-model parameters and the sample
size confirms this advantage is not

00:05:18.794 --> 00:05:21.122
a quirk of one setting but a robust,

00:05:21.202 --> 00:05:22.460
qualitative feature.

00:05:22.840 --> 00:05:24.887
A few numbers capture the impact.

00:05:24.967 --> 00:05:29.591
The moment test needs at least four graphs per
sample just to be defined;

00:05:29.671 --> 00:05:32.362
the cumulant test works with as few as one.

00:05:32.442 --> 00:05:39.580
Both use connected subgraphs up to three edges and
share the same order-n-to-the-omega computational complexity,

00:05:39.660 --> 00:05:40.869
so the gain is free.

00:05:40.949 --> 00:05:42.802
And under the null hypothesis,

00:05:42.882 --> 00:05:49.698
the cumulant statistic hugs a chi-squared
distribution with five degrees of freedom even for small samples,

00:05:49.778 --> 00:05:52.533
where the moment statistic visibly deviates.

00:05:52.613 --> 00:05:58.140
The real networks tested range from about eleven
thousand to sixteen thousand nodes and

00:05:58.220 --> 00:06:00.138
hundreds of thousands of edges,

00:06:00.218 --> 00:06:01.620
all matched in density.

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:04.167
The bottom line is a practical one.

00:06:04.247 --> 00:06:09.430
Whenever you find yourself analyzing networks
through subgraph or motif densities,

00:06:09.510 --> 00:06:13.154
take one extra step and convert them into graph cumulants.

00:06:13.234 --> 00:06:15.657
You lose nothing in computational cost,

00:06:15.737 --> 00:06:18.610
but you gain sharply higher statistical power,

00:06:18.690 --> 00:06:23.232
a trustworthy chi-squared null distribution for
controlling error rates,

00:06:23.312 --> 00:06:27.853
and the ability to draw conclusions even from a
single observed network.

00:06:27.933 --> 00:06:30.742
In short, graph cumulants offer a principled,

00:06:30.822 --> 00:06:36.070
more natural first layer of features for comparing
the local structure of networks.
